!!<*yamlover: $defs: chapter>
The tilde sigil
description: a deprecated back-edge marker on keys and entries; in value position, null as always
- |
  In value position `~` is unchanged — still null (`key: ~`). In **key position** yamlover
  once read a leading `~` as a back-edge sigil: `~key: *P` declared the reverse of the
  forward relation `key`, and `~- *P` a keyless reverse membership. Both are **deprecated
  in favor of path anchors** — `~key: *P` ≡ `&: P: key`, and `~- *P` ≡ `&: P[]` — still
  parsed through the migration window, serialized as anchors only.
- |
  In YAML, `~key:` is simply the plain-scalar key "~key" and `~-` a rare plain scalar —
  another place the same token reads differently, and the reason a literal `~` in a
  yamlover key is escaped: `\~key`.
